{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the names of the children of Israel.</b> The reason He said ״וְאֵלֶּה״ with the addition of the vav is to teach about them that all of them were righteous like their fathers — ״וְאֵלֶּה״ adds to the earlier ones: just as the earlier ones — Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — were supreme righteous, so the children did like the deeds of the fathers." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the way of Amram, of blessed memory (Tanḥuma, Shemot 4), that the time of exile began from the days of Abraham from when Isaac was born. And that is what He said: ״וְאֵלֶּה״ etc. ״הַבָּאִים מִצְרָיְמָה״ — it adds to the earlier ones, to tell you that the earlier ones too were in exile, and these were with them." "<b>Further,</b> it intends: just as the earlier ones knew and recognized [the nature of] the exile, and accepted it upon themselves and upon their seed, so too did these. And that is what He said: ״וְאֵלֶּה״ — adding to the earlier ones." "And according to this approach we have found a sound reason to understand for what purpose the verse went back and counted them, after it had already counted them in parashat VaYigash when they descended to Egypt. And Rashi, of blessed memory, explained that to make known their belovedness He went back and counted them at their death — but this is not sufficient except as a derash and not as the peshat of the matter. And furthermore, according to his own words, He should have fixed the count after saying ״וַיָּמָת יוֹסֵף״ etc. ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת״ etc." "And according to our approach, the verse makes known the reason for the count that it makes and proceeds with throughout the book of the Torah: who and who came to accept the decree of the King, to bear the yoke of exile — and did not turn back, like the deed of the wicked Esau (Genesis 36:8), who went off to a land because of Jacob. And they, of blessed memory, drashed (Genesis Rabbah 82:13): because of the bond of debt of the exile. And He said ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת וְגוֹ׳ הַבָּאִים מִצְרָיְמָה״ — meaning, those who came to bear the yoke of exile in Egypt ״אֶת יַעֲקֹב״ — with him, like him, in one accord to pay off the bond of debt of the exile. Therefore He was precise in saying <b>הַבָּאִים</b> — meaning, even though they had not [yet] come, they were desirous to come. And this is your sign: <b>אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ</b> — like a person who readies himself for the matter. And had they descended to Egypt for a known reason, they would not have uprooted their dwelling and come; rather, they would have come in a temporary manner with their feet set straight to return to the land of Canaan." "And the reason He repeated saying <b>they came</b> and was not satisfied with what He preceded by saying ״הַבָּאִים״, is that He intended to make a distinction between those enumerated in the passage and the people of their households, for the people of their households are not all in this category. And see what I wrote in parashat Vayigash at the verse ״בָּנָיו וּבְנֵי בָנָיו״ etc. (Genesis 46:7), for they specifically were desirous of the matter. And although He enumerated here only the twelve tribes, perhaps they were in a category unto themselves, comparable to Jacob." "<b>Or</b> the verse may be explained in this manner: <b>ואלה שמות</b> the children of Israel — the ones coming of their own will and desire to Egypt — are those registered: Reuben, Simeon, etc.; but all the rest of those who issued from Jacob's thigh, since Jacob came, came with him, and that is what it says: <b>et Jacob, a man and his household, came</b>; and had it not been for Jacob's coming, not all of them would have come. Therefore it was necessary to say ״they came״ a second time." "<b>Further</b>, it intends by saying <b>הַבָּאִים</b> etc., to declare a decisive marvel: that they came of their own free will. And the proof is that after their father died they went up to the land of Canaan and returned a second time, as stated in parashat Vayechi, which is why it says ״הַבָּאִים מִצְרָיְמָה אֵת יַעֲקֹב״ — and then anew ״אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ״: after they went up from there, they returned to Egypt, each man and his household. And Scripture enumerates who were the possessors of this righteousness: Reuben, etc. From this you learn that the branches of the tree are equal to the tree itself in desiring the decree of the King, and it was written in a book of remembrance before Him for those who fear HaShem, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:22.023590629Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}